AU  - Apurva Sanaria
TI  - Family Offices in Entrepreneurial Finance: The Role of Ownership Competence (WITHDRAWN)
JO  - Academy of Management Proceedings
PY  - 2026
AB  - Family offices have become increasingly active investors in entrepreneurial ventures, yet their venture selection preferences remain unclear. Prior research focuses mainly on professional venture capitalists and corporate VC’s emphasizing on their portfolio-based and strategic investment logics. We argue that family offices invest as owners rather than intermediaries, using ownership competence to choose ventures where which they can actively shape. Conceptualizing ownership competence as developed through long-term ownership of operating firms, we examine how it shapes investment choices across four dimensions: industry relatedness, geographic proximity, investment stage, and syndication. Using a novel dataset of Indian family office investments, we find that family offices with higher ownership competence are more likely to invest in industry-related and geographically proximate ventures, to invest at earlier stages, and to invest without syndication. These findings provide evidence that ownership competence influences how family offices apply judgment in venture investment decisions
