AU  - Rose Ji Youn Kim
AU  - Mukund Chari
AU  - Haemin Dennis Park
TI  - Managerial Experience and Discretion in CVC: Determinants of Follow-on Investment Decisions
JO  - Academy of Management Proceedings
PY  - 2026
AB  - Partnerships between established firms and new ventures through corporate venture capital (CVC) have become increasingly prevalent. Because CVC investments are primarily strategic, follow-on investments are critical for realizing corporate investors’ strategic objectives. However, divergent views about partnership value may exist within organizations, making managers central in advocating for ventures and securing internal support. Managers’ investment experience is therefore likely to shape follow-on investment decisions but its influence is ambiguous. Greater investment experience enhances managers’ standing and credibility, increasing their ability to champion the ventures they oversee. However, the experience may heighten managers’ awareness of partnership challenges and prompt them to voice concerns, potentially reducing support. We suggest that managerial discretion clarifies when investment experience influences follow-on investments. When managers possess greater discretion, experience increases follow-on investment because concerns are perceived as manageable rather than deal-breaking. Drawing on the managerial discretion literature, we examine individual, organizational, and external sources of discretion. Using data on 73 CVC managers from 13 firms investing in 366 startups between 2007 and 2017, we find that investment experience increases follow-on investment when (a) managers hold high rank, (b) there has been no CEO turnover at the parent firm, or (c) the portfolio company is younger.
DO  - 10.5465/amproc.2026.12225abstract
UR  - https://doi.org/10.5465/amproc.2026.12225abstract
